-- Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2, batch 2: civilian vehicles and domestic robots.
-- 26 rows (printed 77-96): the Triax commercial S.D.C. automobiles, the
-- Triax hover vehicles, the WR-3030 Bear, the RRK Oberteil and Super-Armored
-- Hover Truck, the WaffenTek BlitzSchnell, and the V-100, V-200, V-250,
-- V-1000 and V-500 domestic robots.
-- See apps/character-creator/docs/surveys/triax-2.md.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
-- EVERY NUMBER WAS READ OFF A RENDER and checked against a render again by
-- book-reconcile; no figure disagreed. Every slug was queried against
-- production vehicles and gear before this file was written.
--
-- CONVENTIONS (as the Warlords of Russia vessels):
--   cost is the lowest printed price; the full wording is in cost_note. "Not
--     available" is NULL with the wording kept.
--   Locations and weapons are in printed order; a small target carries
--     "called shot only" in its mdc_note. Hand to hand tables and sensor
--     notes are in the description.
--   A book slip (a bad metric conversion, a location printed twice, a
--     missile mix that does not add up) is stored as printed and named in
--     the row.
--   S.D.C. machines carry is_mega_damage = 0 and hold S.D.C. in mdc_main_body
--     and their locations (the unit is the flag, not the column name). The
--     book lists the commercial cars under Triax Ground Vehicles with no make,
--     so their names and slugs carry Triax to keep them distinct from other
--     books' generic cars.
--   NOT HERE: the vehicle features and add-on weapon systems (printed 85-89),
--     a price list the survey leaves out.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/add-triax-2-civilian-vehicles.sql

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
('triax-commercial-sdc-automobile', 'Triax Commercial S.D.C. Automobile', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Three passengers comfortably; two more can squeeze in under cramped conditions.', '120 mph (192 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '9-12 feet (2.7 to 3.6 m) long, 5 feet (1.5 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '800-1,000 lbs (360 to 450 kg)', 1500, 0, NULL, 10000, '10,000-14,000 credits for a gasoline engine, 14,000-18,000 for electric, and 60,000 for nuclear.', 'The ordinary civilian car of the NGR, little changed in basic form since the 20th Century apart from styling. Cheap to own and available to any licensed driver. Main body S.D.C. is the book''s equivalent of 15 M.D.C. Range is 500 miles (800 km) per tank of gasoline, 350 miles (560 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear. Has a lunch-box glove compartment and a 3x2x5 foot trunk.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.77'),
('triax-luxury-sdc-sedan', 'Triax Luxury S.D.C. Sedan', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Four passengers comfortably; two more can squeeze in. Luxury limousines seat 4-10 passengers.', '130 mph (208 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '12-20 feet (3.6 to 6.1 m) long, 5 feet (1.5 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '1,200 to 1,800 lbs (540 to 810 kg)', 2000, 0, NULL, 15000, '15,000-25,000 credits for gasoline, 20,000-30,000 for electric, and 70,000 for nuclear; a luxury limousine costs twice as much. Doubling the S.D.C./M.D.C. triples the price.', 'A larger, better-built car with many extra features, driven by Triax executives, celebrities and government officials. Main body S.D.C. equals 20 M.D.C. Range is 350 miles (560 km) on gasoline, 250 miles (400 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear. The limousine version costs twice as much, weighs 50% more and has 30% less range; extra compartments can be added for 2,000-8,000 credits.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.77'),
('triax-sports-car', 'Triax Sports Car', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger; almost always a two-seater.', '200 mph (320 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '9-12 feet (2.7 to 3.6 m) long, 4 feet (1.2 m) tall, 6-7 feet (1.8 to 2.1 m) wide', '1,000-1,500 lbs (450 to 675 kg)', 1500, 0, NULL, 30000, '30,000-55,000 credits for gasoline, 40,000-65,000 electric, and 85,000-100,000 for nuclear.', 'A fast, fine-handling two-seater favored by wealthy NGR businessmen and by street racers in the Republic of Japan; seen as gaudy in the Geofront and rare in North America, where walled cities and bandit-haunted highways leave little room to drive fast. Main body S.D.C. equals 15 M.D.C. Range is 220 miles (352 km) on gasoline, 150 miles (240 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.77'),
('triax-compact-car', 'Triax Compact Car', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger comfortably; a very large passenger will feel cramped.', '120 mph (192 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '7-8 feet (2.1 to 2.4 m) long, 4 feet (1.2 m) tall, 5 feet (1.5 m) wide', '700-900 lbs (315 to 405 kg)', 1200, 0, NULL, 6000, '6,000-10,000 credits for gasoline, 11,000-15,000 for electric, and 50,000 for nuclear (rare).', 'A tiny, economical city car built only by European and Japanese makers and popular with young urbanites; Americans consider it too small. Triax Motorwerks makes the best-selling one, a boxy little model called the "Tiny." Main body S.D.C. equals 12 M.D.C. Range is 300 miles (480 km) on gasoline, 200 miles (320 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear; trunk space is only 2x1x2 feet.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.77-78'),
('triax-motorcycle', 'Triax Motorcycle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Some models seat one passenger; a sidecar (extra 1,000 credits, typically 200 S.D.C.) adds one more passenger or cargo space.', '200 mph (320 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '5-8 feet (1.5 to 2.4 m) long, 3 feet (0.9 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide', '200-600 lbs (90 to 270 kg)', 600, 0, NULL, 3000, '3,000-4,000 credits for gasoline, 7,000-10,000 for electric, and 45,000 for nuclear (very rare). Doubling the S.D.C./M.D.C. triples the price.', 'Motorcycles come in many styles; North Americans, especially in the Pecos Badlands, favor choppers, while Europeans and Asians prefer sleek streamlined bikes. Main body is 600-800 S.D.C. (6-8 M.D.C.); the low end is stored. Range is 300 miles (480 km) on gasoline, 200 miles (320 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with a (very rare) nuclear engine.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.78'),
('triax-motor-scooter', 'Triax Motor Scooter', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Most models carry one passenger who holds on to the driver.', '90 mph (144 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '4-5 feet (1.2 to 1.5 m) long, 3 feet (0.9 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide', '100-200 lbs (45 to 90 kg)', 400, 0, NULL, 1200, '1,200-2,500 credits for gasoline or 4,000-6,000 for electric. No nuclear model. Doubling the S.D.C./M.D.C. triples the price.', 'A small, light motorbike made for city driving, common throughout the NGR and Europe. Main body is 400-500 S.D.C. (4-5 M.D.C.); the low end is stored. Range is 200 miles (320 km) on a small gas tank or 150 miles (240 km) on one battery charge; no nuclear engine is offered. Lunch-box sized compartments, bags and sacks can be attached.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.78'),
('triax-pickup-truck', 'Triax Pickup Truck', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger in front; larger models seat 2-3 more behind them for an added 3,000 credits.', '120 mph (192 km).', NULL, NULL, '14-18 feet (4.3 to 5.4 m) long, 7-9 feet (2.1 to 2.7 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '2,000-3,000 lbs (900 to 1,350 kg)', 2500, 0, NULL, 12000, '12,000-15,000 credits for gasoline, 15,000-20,000 electric, and 60,000 for nuclear.', 'A utility truck with a rear flatbed, built for hauling and towing; Triax models include an electric winch. Main body S.D.C. equals 25 M.D.C. The 7x5x2 foot bed carries up to 3,500 lbs (1,575 kg) and it can pull up to six tons. Range is 250 miles (400 km) on gasoline, 180 miles (288 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.78'),
('triax-jeeps-and-suvs', 'Triax Jeeps & SUVs', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger in front; three more can sit in the back for an added 3,000 credits.', '120 mph (192 km).', NULL, NULL, '12-16 feet (3.6 to 4.9 m) long, 6-7 feet (1.8 to 2.1 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '2,000-4,000 lbs (900 to 1,800 kg)', 3200, 0, NULL, 15000, '15,000-20,000 credits for gasoline, 20,000-30,000 electric, and 65,000 for nuclear.', 'Rugged off-road vehicles for broken roads and rough country. Little used in the NGR, where people avoid the dangerous countryside, but popular in North America, especially M.D.C. versions with a 100-125 M.D.C. main body, among those who cannot afford hover vehicles. Main body S.D.C. equals 32 M.D.C. Range is 250 miles (400 km) on gasoline, 180 miles (288 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear; there is a small 3 by 5 foot flatbed in back.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.78-79'),
('triax-cargo-van', 'Triax Cargo Van', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger comfortably.', '100 mph (160 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '15-20 feet (4.6 to 6.1 m) long, 8-10 feet (2.4 to 3 m) tall, 7 feet (2.1 m) wide', '3,000 to 4,500 lbs (1350 to 2025 kg)', 3500, 0, NULL, 14000, '14,000-20,000 credits for gasoline, 19,000-30,000 for electric, and 60,000 for nuclear.', 'A cargo van or small paneled truck used by couriers, traveling merchants and delivery services; the biggest can carry power armor, and the same stats serve for ambulances. Main body S.D.C. equals 35 M.D.C. The rear 80% is cargo space and it hauls or pulls up to 6,000 lbs (2700 kg). Range is 250 miles (400 km) on gasoline, 180 miles (288 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.79'),
('triax-commercial-bus', 'Triax Commercial Bus', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', '40-50 passengers comfortably depending on design and size; another 20-25 can be squeezed in.', '100 mph (160 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '30-35 feet (9.1 to 10.7 m) long, 10-12 feet (3 to 3.6 m) tall, 8 feet (2.4 m) wide', '5,000 to 6,000 lbs (2,250 to 2,700 kg)', 7000, 0, NULL, 30000, '30,000-45,000 credits for gasoline, 40,000-55,000 for electric, and 85,000-90,000 for nuclear.', 'A passenger bus used by public transit, schools, churches, tour companies and private industry; public systems charge a credit or two a ride and mostly run electric engines. It can also move workers, soldiers, prisoners or supplies. Main body S.D.C. equals 70 M.D.C. Long-distance models have an undercarriage hold for up to 2.5 tons of baggage. Range is 300 miles (480 km) on gasoline, 200 miles (320 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.79'),
('triax-armored-security-vehicle', 'Triax Armored Security Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger in front, typically a guard riding shotgun. Only models built to carry personnel or prisoners have seats in the rear.', '100 mph (160 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '20 feet (6.1 m) long, 10 feet (3 m) tall, 7 feet (2.1 m) wide', 'about five tons', 200, 1, NULL, 200000, '200,000 for gasoline, 230,000 for electric, and 260,000 for nuclear; add 30% for military grade vehicles. Economy version: half the M.D.C. throughout, at half the price in every category.', 'An armored car for moving money, valuables, data, artifacts, munitions, hazardous materials and important people or prisoners; it is the only civilian vehicle whose armor may legally be upgraded to Mega-Damage alloys (except in the CS). Unlike the other commercial vehicles it is an M.D.C. machine: main body 200 M.D.C., or 300 for the military model. The rear 80% is cargo space carrying up to 10 tons, with optional folding shelves and tie-straps. Range is 220 miles (352 km) on gasoline, 150 miles (240 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.79-80'),
('triax-16-wheel-cargo-truck-and-trailer', 'Triax 16-Wheel Cargo Truck & Trailer', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger in front; some have a bigger cab for 2-3 more, and most have a two-bunk sleeping compartment.', '100 mph (160 km).', NULL, NULL, 'Trailer 26-40 feet (7.9 to 12.2 m) long, 15 feet (4.6 m) tall, 7-9 feet (2.1 to 2.7 m) wide', '10-12 tons empty (truck and trailer)', 8000, 0, NULL, 90000, 'Truck and trailer: 90,000-120,000 for gasoline, 140,000-150,000 for electric, and 190,000-220,000 for nuclear.', 'A big-rig truck and trailer much like those of pre-Rifts days, only sleeker, common in the NGR, Poland, Russia and on North American trade routes such as the Arzno Great Trade Road. It hauls 65,000 to 90,000 lbs (29,250 to 40,500 kg). There is no single Main Body line: the truck/cab has 8,000 S.D.C. (equal to 80 M.D.C., stored as the main body) and the trailer 5,000 (50 M.D.C.). Range is 400 miles (640 km) on gasoline, 300 miles (480 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.80'),
('triax-hovercycle', 'Triax Hovercycle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger sits snugly behind and holds on to the driver; no sidecar.', '200 mph (320 km).', NULL, NULL, '6-9 feet (1.8 to 2.7 m) long, 3 feet (0.9 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide', '500-700 lbs (225 to 315 kg)', 800, 0, NULL, 20000, '20,000-25,000 credits for electric, and 55,000-65,000 credits for nuclear. Doubling the S.D.C./M.D.C. triples the price.', 'A quick, nimble hover bike, inexpensive as hover vehicles go. Main body S.D.C. equals 8 M.D.C. Range is 200 miles (320 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear; saddlebag-sized compartments can be attached. Like all commercial NGR hover vehicles it is built of S.D.C. materials, rides 1-3 feet (0.3 to 0.9 m) above the ground (4-6 feet in an emergency) and can cross rough terrain and shallow water; a car driver without hover training is -40% to pilot it and -60% on stunts and control rolls.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.80-81'),
('triax-a-type-hover-car', 'Triax A-Type Hover Car', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Three passengers comfortably; 1-2 more can squeeze in.', '130 mph (208 km).', NULL, NULL, '10-14 feet (3 to 4.2 m) long, 5 feet (1.5 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '1,000-1,200 lbs (450 to 540 kg)', 1600, 0, NULL, 60000, '60,000-70,000 for electric, and 90,000-120,000 for nuclear.', 'Triax''s affordable everyday hover car, sold in dozens of models and making up 51% of its auto sales. Any dealer modification costs 25% less on an A-Type. It comes equipped with radio, a two-disc CD player, digital gyro-compass, clock and calendar, voice-activated ignition and locks, and an anti-theft siren and alarm. Main body S.D.C. equals 16 M.D.C.; range 160 miles (256 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear. Like all commercial NGR hover vehicles it is built of S.D.C. materials, rides 1-3 feet (0.3 to 0.9 m) above the ground (4-6 feet in an emergency) and can cross rough terrain and shallow water; a car driver without hover training is -40% to pilot it and -60% on stunts and control rolls.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.81'),
('triax-z-type-luxury-hover-sedan', 'Triax Z-Type Luxury Hover Sedan', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Four passengers comfortably, six cramped; limousines (16-20 feet/4.9 to 6.1 m long) seat 4-10.', '170 mph (272 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '12-18 feet (3.6 to 5.5 m) long, 5 feet (1.5 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '1,300-1,800 lbs (585 to 810 kg)', 2200, 0, NULL, 85000, '85,000-110,000 for electric, and 140,000-160,000 for nuclear. A six battery system (240 mile range) costs 25,000 more.', 'Triax''s prestige hover sedan for the rich and famous, fast and fine-handling. Its active hover suspension adds +5% to Piloting skill rolls, and it carries a dashboard computer with NGR-wide broadband, a 10-disc CD player, sound proofing, and a full environmental seal with climate control and 6 hours of independent air. Limousine versions also get a sunroof, spotlight, deluxe sound system, printers and onboard computers, and a bar. Main body S.D.C. equals 22 M.D.C.; range 160 miles (256 km) per charge on four batteries, unlimited with nuclear. Like all commercial NGR hover vehicles it is built of S.D.C. materials, rides 1-3 feet (0.3 to 0.9 m) above the ground (4-6 feet in an emergency) and can cross rough terrain and shallow water; a car driver without hover training is -40% to pilot it and -60% on stunts and control rolls.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.81'),
('triax-s-type-hover-sports-car', 'Triax S-Type Hover Sports Car', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger.', '230 mph (368 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '9-13 feet (2.7 to 3.9 m) long, 5 feet (1.5 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', '1,100-1,400 lbs (495 to 630 kg)', 1800, 0, NULL, 75000, '75,000-100,000 for electric, and 130,000-150,000 for nuclear. A six battery system (240 mile range) costs 25,000 more.', 'A sleek, very fast two-seat hover sports car, the alternative to the Z-Type for wealthy buyers. Its active hover suspension gives +5% to Piloting skill rolls, and it carries a dashboard computer with broadband, a 5-disc CD player and a full environmental seal with climate control and 6 hours of independent air. Main body S.D.C. equals 18 M.D.C.; range 160 miles (256 km) per charge, unlimited with nuclear. The trunk is printed as "2x2x3 foot (0.6 x 0.6 x 0.9 foot)", a metric slip. Like all commercial NGR hover vehicles it is built of S.D.C. materials, rides 1-3 feet (0.3 to 0.9 m) above the ground (4-6 feet in an emergency) and can cross rough terrain and shallow water; a car driver without hover training is -40% to pilot it and -60% on stunts and control rolls.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.81-82'),
('triax-b-type-hover-work-vehicle', 'Triax B-Type Hover Work Vehicles', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger standard; a third can slide in under cramped conditions.', '130 mph (208 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, '18-26 feet (5.5 to 7.9 m) long, 7-12 feet (2.1 to 3.6 m) tall, 7-8 feet (2.1 to 2.4 m) wide', '2-3 tons', 3200, 0, NULL, 290000, '290,000-320,000 credits for electric and 370,000-450,000 for nuclear. A six battery system (180 mile range) costs 25,000 more.', 'Triax''s line of hover work vehicles: cargo vans, small trucks and hover pickups with large beds. The rear 80% is cargo space carrying 8-12 tons depending on size, and heavy loads drain power faster. Standard features include alarm, voice-activated locks, a dashboard computer with broadband, a five-disc CD player and an active hover suspension giving +5% to Piloting skill rolls. Main body S.D.C. equals 32 M.D.C.; range 120 miles (192 km) per charge, unlimited with nuclear. Like all commercial NGR hover vehicles it is built of S.D.C. materials, rides 1-3 feet (0.3 to 0.9 m) above the ground (4-6 feet in an emergency) and can cross rough terrain and shallow water; a car driver without hover training is -40% to pilot it and -60% on stunts and control rolls.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.82'),
('wr-3030-bear-all-terrain-vehicle', 'WR-3030 "Bear" All-Terrain Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One pilot.', 'One additional passenger.', '150 mph (240 km) maximum.', NULL, NULL, 'Length 8 feet (2.4 m)', '1,200 lbs (540 kg)', 60, 1, NULL, 135000, 'Market cost: 135,000 credits for a gasoline engine, 145,000 for electric, and 196,000 for nuclear. The Power System line instead says nuclear costs an additional 1.4 million credits; both are printed.', 'A fast, rugged quad-wheeled ATV the size of a motorcycle (German name Der Bar), part of Triax''s WR Wilderness Class of light M.D.C. vehicles that are sold for delivery outside the NGR and illegal inside it. Popular with wilderness folk, small bandit gangs, Gypsies and rural youth; it handles forest trails, mud and mountain passes as well as pavement. Range is 500 miles (800 km) on gasoline, 350 miles (560 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear. No cargo; weapons and sensors are optional and rarely fitted, and up to 50 more main body M.D.C. can be added at 25,000 credits per 10. The book adds that any vehicle in the preceding pages can be bought as a WR M.D.C. version for its price plus 2,000 credits per main body M.D.C. (50 minimum; 220 maximum for hovercycles and cars, 320 for trucks bigger than a pickup).', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.82-83'),
('rrk-oberteil-armored-hovercar', 'RRK Oberteil Armored Hovercar', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Four passengers comfortably, six squeezed in tight.', '150 mph (240 km).', NULL, NULL, '14 feet (4.3 m) long, 5 feet (1.5 m) tall, 6 feet (1.8 m) wide', 'two tons', 220, 1, NULL, 410000, '410,000 for electric, and 475,000 for nuclear.', 'An M.D.C. armored hover sedan from RRK (Rustung-An-Der-Rhein Korporation of Koln), Europe''s leading maker of commercial armored vehicles. It is sold unarmed but RRK will add to order six non-weapon features and as many as three weapon systems. Illegal in the NGR without a rare government permit, it is sold abroad and through the Black Market. Comes with radio, digital gyro-compass, clock and calendar, keyed ignition and locks, and a siren and alarm. Range 160 miles (256 km) on one battery charge, and without limit with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.83-84'),
('rrk-super-armored-hover-truck', 'RRK Super-Armored Hover Truck', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger comfortably; an expanded passenger compartment (add 25,000 credits, cargo space -15%) holds three more comfortably, four cramped.', '100 mph (160 km).', NULL, NULL, '25 feet (7.6 m) long, 10 feet (3 m) tall, 8 feet (2.4 m) wide', '10 tons', 525, 1, NULL, 2000000, '2 million credits for electric, and 2.3 million for nuclear.', 'A hover truck from RRK armored as heavily as some tanks, popular with security firms, banks, art and artifact collectors, wilderness merchants, mercenaries and Gypsies, and illegal inside the NGR. 80% of it is cargo space, and the cargo room''s environment is controlled separately; it also has an alarm, voice-activated locks, and a remote lockdown so the driver can kill the engine if the truck is stolen. Eight non-weapon features and as many as five weapon systems can be added. Range 120 miles (192 km) on one battery charge because of its weight, unlimited with nuclear.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.84'),
('waffentek-blitzschnell', 'WaffenTek BlitzSchnell', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One passenger only.', '275 mph (440 km).', NULL, NULL, '11 feet (3.3 m) long, 4 feet (1.4 m) tall, 7 feet (2.1 m) wide', '1,000-1,200 lbs (450 to 540 kg)', 160, 1, NULL, 379000, '379,000 for electric, and 435,000 for nuclear.', 'WaffenTek''s flagship M.D.C. hover car ("lightning fast"), built for raw speed and acceleration and close to some military vehicles, a rival to the WR 2020 Shark Bullet Bike from Triax. Its responsive design gives +5% to Piloting skill rolls. Range is 160 miles (256 km) per charge below 200 mph (320 km) but 120 miles (192 km) when driven faster; unlimited with nuclear. Equipment includes a two-disc CD player, gyro-compass, voice-activated locks and alarm; five non-weapon features and as many as two weapon systems can be added. Illegal inside the NGR. Height is printed "4 feet (1.4 m)", a conversion slip (4 feet is 1.2 m).', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.84-85'),
('triax-v-100-housekeeper', 'Triax V-100 Housekeeper', 'rifts', 'robot', 'None; a simple artificial intelligence.', NULL, 'Running 15 mph (24 km) maximum; cannot leap or fly.', NULL, NULL, '5 feet, 8 inches (1.7 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide, 2 feet (0.6 m) long', '250 lbs (112.5 kg)', 90, 0, 14, 350000, '350,000 credits new; as little as 150,000 used. Black Market "refurbished" (often stolen) units go for as low as 90,000 but are 1D4+4 years old (-20% S.D.C., -10% skill programs).', 'A household robot built as a butler (V-100M) or housemaid (V-100F) that cooks, cleans, mends clothes and greets guests; owners often dress its clunky painted-on servant uniform in real clothes. Robotic P.S. 15, I.Q. 10, P.P. 12, P.B. 7, Spd 22. Its standard Domestic Services program covers First Aid, Housekeeping, Language and Literacy: Euro, Preserve Food, Radio: Basic, Recycling, Sewing and Wardrobe & Grooming at 88-92% (30,000 credits as an add-on). Optional Bartender, Culinary, Language and Entertainment programs (3,000-35,000 credits) and upgrades such as a language translator or Human Personality Emulation (200,000) are available. Like all Triax domestic robots it is built of S.D.C. metals rather than M.D.C. materials, has no combat programming, gets four actions per melee round, and runs on a proprietary battery good for 60-80 hours that any Triax dealer recharges for 100 credits in an hour.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.90-91'),
('triax-v-200-robot-chauffeur', 'Triax V-200 Robot Chauffeur', 'rifts', 'robot', 'None; a simple artificial intelligence.', NULL, 'Running 15 mph (24 km) maximum; cannot leap or fly.', NULL, NULL, '5 feet, 8 inches (1.7 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide, 2 feet (0.6 m) long', '250 lbs (112.5 kg)', 90, 0, 14, 450000, '450,000 credits new; as little as 199,000 used. Black Market "refurbished" units go for as low as 140,000 but are 1D4+4 years old (-20% S.D.C., -10% skill programs).', 'A robot driver that also repairs and cleans the vehicles in its care, used by frequent travelers, the ultra-rich, some taxi and limousine firms, and shipping and courier companies. It drives safely and cannot be ordered into stunts or vehicle combat. Robotic P.S. 15, I.Q. 12, P.P. 15, P.B. 7, Spd 22. Standard programs: Basic Ground Vehicles piloting (Pilot: Automobile 96%, Hovercraft and Motorcycle 90%, Truck 80%) and a Computerized Atlas of NGR and Polish streets; add-ons cover aircraft and auto mechanics and specialty aircraft and craft piloting (25,000-55,000 credits). It has a wide band radio, optical scanner and motion detector. Like all Triax domestic robots it is built of S.D.C. metals rather than M.D.C. materials, has no combat programming, gets four actions per melee round, and runs on a proprietary battery good for 60-80 hours that any Triax dealer recharges for 100 credits in an hour.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.91-92'),
('triax-v-250-landscaper-farm-worker', 'Triax V-250 Landscaper/Farm Worker', 'rifts', 'robot', 'None; a simple artificial intelligence.', NULL, 'Running 15 mph (24 km) maximum; cannot leap or fly.', NULL, NULL, '6 feet (1.8 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide, 2 feet (0.6 m) long', '300 lbs (135 kg)', 120, 0, 14, 430000, '430,000 credits new for a basic model; as little as 200,000 used. Black Market "refurbished" units go for as low as 145,000 but are 1D4+4 years old (-20% S.D.C., -10% skill programs).', 'Originally an agricultural robot that plants, plows, picks and harvests without rest; after a fad for its many attachments faded it found a second life tending estates, parks and campuses, and aging units still work fields across Europe. Robotic P.S. 15, I.Q. 10, P.P. 12, P.B. 7, Spd 22. The buyer picks a Landscaping or a Farm Labor program (the other costs 22,000 or 67,000), and it has a chemical sprayer and macro eye. Modular tool arms replace a hand: chain saw 3D6+10 S.D.C., circular saw 2D6+10, drill 1D6+4, cutting laser 2D6+10 S.D.C. to 60 feet (18.3 m), scissor fingers 1D4, plus baskets, blades, sprayers and a thresher (600-18,000 credits); an extra pair of arms costs 375,000. Like all Triax domestic robots it is built of S.D.C. metals rather than M.D.C. materials, has no combat programming, gets four actions per melee round, and runs on a proprietary battery good for 60-80 hours that any Triax dealer recharges for 100 credits in an hour.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.92-93'),
('triax-v-1000-personal-assistant-bot', 'Triax V-1000 Personal Assistant-Bot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'None; a simple artificial intelligence.', NULL, 'Running 31 mph (49.6 km) maximum; cannot leap or fly.', NULL, NULL, '6 feet (1.8 m) tall, 2 feet (0.6 m) wide, 2 feet (0.6 m) long', '250 lbs (112.5 kg)', 120, 0, 14, 600000, '600,000 credits new; as little as 320,000 used. Black Market "refurbished" units go for as low as 225,000 but are 1D4+4 years old (-20% S.D.C., -10% skill programs).', 'An all-purpose personal assistant robot that takes eight skill programs in total, where most domestic robots take four additional programs. Robotic P.S. 15, I.Q. 15, P.P. 12, P.B. 7, Spd 31. It comes with General Knowledge and Tutorial programs; add-on specializations (Adventuring, Commerce, Computers, Medical Assistant, Physical Trainer, Science, Security, Technical) cost 40,000-120,000 credits, the Physical Trainer program giving +2 attacks per melee, and any other domestic program costs 1D4x10,000+15,000 if unpriced. Upgrades include a Bio-Scan Medical Survey Unit (300,000) and Human Personality Emulation (400,000). Like all Triax domestic robots it is built of S.D.C. metals rather than M.D.C. materials, has no combat programming, gets four actions per melee round, and runs on a proprietary battery good for 60-80 hours that any Triax dealer recharges for 100 credits in an hour.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.93-95'),
('triax-v-500-robot-pet', 'Triax V-500 Robot Pet', 'rifts', 'robot', 'None; a simple artificial intelligence.', NULL, 'Running 20 mph (32 km) maximum; leaps 3 feet (0.9 m) up or across, double with a running start; cannot fly.', NULL, NULL, 'Up to 3 feet (0.9 m) tall, up to one foot (0.3 m) wide, up to 3 feet (0.9 m) long', 'Up to 60 lbs (27 kg)', 35, 0, 15, 20000, 'Small to medium-sized pets: 20,000-35,000 credits new. Large pets: 45,000-55,000 new. A used or refurbished model sells for one third to one half of new (1D4+4 years old; -20% S.D.C., -10% skill programs). Lifelike fur or feathers cost extra.', 'Robotic house pets made as cats, dogs, ferrets, pigs, lizards, monkeys and large birds (which cannot fly) that read and respond to their owners'' emotions and bark an alarm at intruders. Robotic P.S. is 12 for large pets and 8 for small and medium ones; I.Q. 10, P.P. 14, P.B. 10, Spd 30. The Pet Simulation and Human Personality Emulation programs are standard; add-ons include Hunter/Tracker (15,000), Medical Helper (75,000) and Police Dog (250,000) programs, and realistic fur and eyes. The book prints one stat block for every animal. Like all Triax domestic robots it is built of S.D.C. metals rather than M.D.C. materials, has no combat programming, gets four actions per melee round, and runs on a proprietary battery good for 60-80 hours that any Triax dealer recharges for 100 credits in an hour.', 'Rifts World Book 31: Triax 2 p.95-96');

